From: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
Subject: The SCTP packets of one usrsctp call leave in batched sends

usrsctp hands each packet to the write callback on its own, and each became one DTLS record and one sendto: a data
channel at full rate spent a fifth of its CPU in those system calls. While a BurstScope is open on a thread,
DtlsTransport collects its records there, and the outermost scope sends them through juice_send_batch (UDP_SEGMENT),
32 at a time. SctpTransport opens a scope around each call into usrsctp that sends: a send, a flush, and the input of
a received packet (a SACK lets the next DATA out); DtlsTransport opens one over the records it drains in one wake,
so the DATA that several SACKs let out leaves together. usrsctp's own timer thread opens none, so a retransmission
or a heartbeat still leaves at once.

Nothing is sent before the scope ends, and every scope is opened before its lock is taken: the send, and its wait
for a full socket, run after the SCTP and DTLS locks are released. The layer below tells how many records the
socket took (sendBatchPrefix). The SCTP stack was already told that all of them were sent, so the ones not taken are
kept and given again, without the ones taken, until one second after the first shortfall; a transport that cannot
send any more ends the burst at once. What is given up is counted in UnsentMediaDatagrams and logged.
